from full_a import C
LONG = {}

LONG["josiah"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Josiah was", """Josiah was king of Judah, the southern kingdom, from about 640 to 609 BC. He became king at only eight years old. His father, King Amon, was killed by his own officials after ruling just two years, and the people put the boy on the throne. His grandfather was Manasseh, who ruled 55 years and was one of the most wicked kings Judah ever had. Manasseh built altars to foreign gods in the Temple itself and even sacrificed his own son.

So Josiah grew up in a kingdom full of idols, with a royal family that had turned away from the Lord.""", ("2 Kings", 22)),
("Why his story matters", """Josiah’s story is about a young person who chose to follow God even though everything around him pulled the other way. It is also about what happens when God’s Word is rediscovered after being lost and ignored.""")],
cards=[
C("A boy who sought God", "At sixteen Josiah began to seek the God of David, and at twenty he began to tear down the idols.",
"""The Bible says that in the eighth year of his reign, when he was still a boy of about sixteen, Josiah began to seek the God of his ancestor David. Four years later, at twenty, he began to clean up the land. He had the high places, the Asherah poles and the carved and cast idols torn down. He ordered the altars of the Baals to be smashed in his presence and the incense altars above them cut down. He ground the idols to dust and scattered it over the graves of those who had sacrificed to them.

It took courage. These altars had stood for generations. The priests and officials who profited from them were still in power. A young king still in his early twenties took on the whole system.""", ("2 Chronicles", 34)),
C("The lost book", "While repairing the Temple, the high priest found the Book of the Law. Josiah tore his clothes when it was read to him.",
"""When Josiah was 26, he ordered the Temple repaired. It had been neglected and damaged under the idol-worshiping kings. Workers were paid and carpenters and builders were hired. While they were working, Hilkiah the high priest found the Book of the Law of the Lord. It was probably the book of Deuteronomy, or at least a large part of the Law of Moses. Somehow it had been lost in the Temple of all places.

A secretary named Shaphan read it aloud to the king. As Josiah heard the words, especially the warnings of what would happen if Israel turned away from God, he tore his clothes in grief. “Great is the wrath of the Lord that is poured out on us, because our fathers have not obeyed the words of this book.” He realized how far the whole nation had drifted from what God had said.""", ("2 Kings", 22)),
C("The prophetess Huldah", "Josiah sent to ask what would happen, and the prophetess Huldah told him judgment would come, but not in his lifetime.",
"""Josiah sent his officials to ask the Lord what they should do. They went to a prophetess named Huldah, who lived in Jerusalem. Her answer had two parts. First, God would indeed bring disaster on Jerusalem, as the book warned, because the people had abandoned Him. Second, because Josiah’s heart was tender, because he humbled himself and wept before God, he would be gathered to his grave in peace and not see the disaster.

It is worth noting that the king, the high priest and the secretary chose to ask a woman prophet for God’s word, and the Bible treats her answer with full respect. Josiah did not simply sit back and accept that disaster would come later. He kept leading the nation back to God as hard as he could.""", ("2 Kings", 22)),
C("The covenant and the cleanup", "Josiah read the whole book to the people, renewed the covenant, and removed every idol in the land.",
"""Josiah called all the elders, priests, prophets and people, from the least to the greatest, up to the Temple. He read the entire Book of the Covenant aloud to them. Then he stood by the pillar and made a covenant before the Lord: to follow Him and keep His commands with all his heart and soul. All the people joined in.

Then came the great cleanup. He removed everything built for Baal and Asherah from the Temple, burned it and scattered the ashes. He shut down the pagan priests, tore down the places of male prostitution connected to worship, and destroyed the place in the valley where children were sacrificed to the god Molech. He went north into the old territory of Israel, to the altar at Bethel that Jeroboam had set up 300 years earlier, and broke it down. That was exactly what a prophet had foretold, even naming Josiah, before Josiah was born.""", ("2 Kings", 23)),
C("The greatest Passover", "Josiah led the biggest Passover celebration since the days of Samuel. Then he died in battle at Megiddo.",
"""Josiah commanded the people to celebrate the Passover, the yearly meal remembering the rescue from Egypt, which had long been neglected. The Bible says no Passover like it had been held since the days of the prophet Samuel. Josiah himself gave thousands of lambs and goats and cattle for the people to use.

The Bible gives Josiah very high praise: “Before him there was no king like him, who turned to the Lord with all his heart and with all his soul and with all his might, according to all the Law of Moses.”

But in 609 BC, the Egyptian king Pharaoh Necho marched north to help Assyria against the rising power of Babylon. Josiah went out to stop him at Megiddo. He was hit by archers and died of his wounds in Jerusalem. All of Judah mourned, and the prophet Jeremiah wrote a lament for him. About twenty years later, Babylon destroyed Jerusalem, just as Huldah had said.""", ("2 Chronicles", 35))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Josiah shows that no one is too young to follow God, and that a family’s history does not have to decide your future. His grandfather was a terrible king, and Josiah was one of the best.

His story also shows what the Bible does in a life. When he heard God’s Word, he did not just admire it. He changed what he was doing. The book had been in the Temple the whole time, and nobody noticed. We may have a Bible on a shelf or phone and still need to open it and listen.

And Josiah’s sorrow at God’s warning was not wasted. God saw his humble heart. The Bible says that God notices when people take His words seriously.""", ("2 Kings", 22))])

LONG["miriam"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Miriam was", """Miriam was the older sister of Moses and Aaron. She was born in Egypt, in the time when the Israelites were slaves and the king had ordered every newborn Hebrew boy to be thrown into the Nile. She is the first woman in the Bible called a prophet.

She was probably about ten or twelve years old when her baby brother was born. Her whole family lived under the threat of death, and she was part of a plan that saved Moses’ life.""", ("Exodus", 2)),
("Why her story matters", """Miriam shows up at three key moments: when Moses was a baby, when Israel crossed the sea, and when she made a serious mistake. Her story is about courage, praise, and being forgiven.""")],
cards=[
C("The girl by the river", "When baby Moses was set afloat in a basket, his sister stood at a distance to see what would happen.",
"""Moses’ mother hid him for three months. When she could hide him no longer, she made a small waterproof basket of papyrus, coated it with tar, put the baby inside and set it among the reeds at the edge of the Nile. The Bible says his sister “stood at a distance to know what would be done to him.” Tradition has said that this sister was Miriam, though the Bible does not name her here.

Pharaoh’s daughter came to bathe, saw the basket and had pity on the crying baby. “This is one of the Hebrews’ children,” she said. Then Moses’ sister stepped forward and asked, “Shall I go and call you a nurse from the Hebrew women to nurse the child for you?” The princess said, “Go.” The girl brought her own mother. Pharaoh’s daughter said, “Take this child away and nurse him for me, and I will give you your wages.”

By being brave and quick, Miriam kept her brother with his own family and raised in the palace, in the very household of the king who wanted him dead.""", ("Exodus", 2)),
C("The song at the sea", "After crossing the Red Sea, Miriam took a tambourine and led the women in a song of victory.",
"""Years later, Moses led Israel out of Egypt. Pharaoh’s army chased them to the shore of the Red Sea. God opened the sea, the Israelites crossed on dry land, and the water closed over the Egyptian chariots. Moses and the people sang a long song of praise to the Lord.

Then the Bible says: “Miriam the prophetess, the sister of Aaron, took a tambourine in her hand, and all the women went out after her with tambourines and dancing. And Miriam sang to them: ‘Sing to the Lord, for He has triumphed gloriously; the horse and his rider He has thrown into the sea.’”

Miriam would have been about 85 or 90 years old at the time. The Bible’s first picture of worship after the rescue shows a woman leading the people in joyful singing and dancing. The prophet Micah later listed her along with Moses and Aaron as one of the three leaders God sent to bring Israel out of Egypt.""", ("Exodus", 15)),
C("A mistake", "In the wilderness, Miriam and Aaron spoke against Moses, and God struck Miriam with a skin disease.",
"""Some time later, Miriam and Aaron began to talk against Moses because of the Cushite woman he had married. Then they said, “Has the Lord indeed spoken only through Moses? Has He not spoken through us also?” The Bible adds a remarkable comment: Moses was “very meek, more than all people who were on the face of the earth.”

The Lord heard them. He called all three to the tent of meeting and came down in a pillar of cloud. He explained that Moses was different: God spoke to him “mouth to mouth,” clearly, not in riddles. “Why then were you not afraid to speak against my servant Moses?” When the cloud lifted, Miriam’s skin was covered with a white disease, “like snow.”

Aaron begged Moses, “Oh, my lord, do not punish us because we have done foolishly.” And Moses did something kind: he prayed, “O God, please heal her.” God said she must be shut outside the camp for seven days, like a person with a skin disease. And the whole people of Israel waited and did not move on until she was brought back.""", ("Numbers", 12)),
C("Her death", "Miriam died at Kadesh in the wilderness and was buried there.",
"""Near the end of the forty years in the wilderness, the Israelites came to a place called Kadesh in the desert of Zin. The Bible says, in one short verse, “Miriam died there and was buried there.” There is no long speech and no long account of her last words, only a quiet record that she was gone.

The very next verse says there was no water for the people, and they grumbled again. Moses, grieving and tired, struck a rock in anger instead of speaking to it as God had told him. That moment cost Moses the chance of entering the promised land. Many have wondered if the loss of his sister, who had been beside him from the very beginning, was part of what he was carrying.""", ("Numbers", 20))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Miriam’s life is long, and it has high points and low points. She was brave as a young girl, joyful as a worship leader, and wrong when she let jealousy talk. The Bible does not hide her failure, and it also does not forget her as a leader.

The best part of her story is what happened after she was wrong. Moses prayed for her, God healed her, and the whole nation waited for her to come back. God’s people do not leave someone behind who has failed and is sorry.

It also reminds us that worship does not need a crowd or a stage. Miriam’s tambourine and her song were part of the Bible’s record of what God did. Praise is a good answer to what God has done for us.""", ("Micah", 6))])

LONG["hannah"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Hannah was", """Hannah lived in the hill country of Ephraim around 1100 BC, in the time of the judges, a period of Israel’s history when, as the Bible puts it, “everyone did what was right in his own eyes.” Her husband Elkanah loved her deeply. But Hannah had a great sorrow: she could not have children. In that culture, a woman with no children often felt shame and was looked down on.

Elkanah also had another wife, Peninnah, who had many children and who made a habit of taunting Hannah.""", ("1 Samuel", 1)),
("Why her story matters", """Hannah’s prayer in Shiloh is one of the best-known prayers in the Bible. Her story is about bringing real pain to God, and about what happens when we give back to Him what He gives us.""")],
cards=[
C("Years of sorrow", "Each year at Shiloh, Peninnah teased Hannah until she cried and would not eat.",
"""Every year the family traveled to Shiloh, where the tabernacle stood, to worship and offer a sacrifice. Elkanah gave Peninnah’s children their portions of the meat. To Hannah he gave a double portion, because he loved her, though “the Lord had closed her womb.”

Peninnah used these trips to hurt Hannah. “Year by year, as often as she went up to the house of the Lord, she used to provoke her. Therefore Hannah wept and would not eat.” Elkanah tried to comfort her: “Hannah, why do you weep? Why do you not eat? Why is your heart sad? Am I not more to you than ten sons?” He meant well, but he did not understand. Her husband’s love did not take away her longing for a child.""", ("1 Samuel", 1)),
C("A prayer with tears", "At the tabernacle, Hannah prayed silently and made a vow. The priest Eli thought she was drunk.",
"""After one of the meals, Hannah got up and went to pray. The priest Eli was sitting by the doorpost of the tabernacle. Hannah was “deeply distressed and prayed to the Lord and wept bitterly.” She made a vow: “O Lord of hosts, if you will indeed look on the affliction of your servant and remember me, and not forget your servant, but will give to your servant a son, then I will give him to the Lord all the days of his life, and no razor shall touch his head.”

She prayed so long, and her lips moved but no sound came out, that Eli thought she was drunk. He scolded her: “How long will you go on being drunk? Put your wine away from you.” Hannah answered, “No, my lord, I am a woman troubled in spirit. I have been pouring out my soul before the Lord.” Eli realized his mistake and said, “Go in peace, and the God of Israel grant your petition that you have made to him.”

The Bible says she went on her way and ate. “And her face was no longer sad.” She had not yet received an answer, but she had given her burden to God.""", ("1 Samuel", 1)),
C("A son named Samuel", "God answered. Hannah gave birth to a boy and named him Samuel.",
"""The next year, Hannah had a son. She named him Samuel, a name that sounds like the Hebrew for “heard by God.” “Because I have asked for him from the Lord.”

When the family went to Shiloh the next year, Hannah stayed home. She told her husband, “After the child is weaned, I will bring him, so that he may appear in the presence of the Lord and dwell there forever.” In that time, children were often nursed for about three years.

When the day came, Hannah brought Samuel to Shiloh with a three-year-old bull, a measure of flour and a skin of wine. She found Eli and said: “Oh, my lord! As you live, my lord, I am the woman who was standing here in your presence, praying to the Lord. For this child I prayed, and the Lord has granted me my petition that I made to him. Therefore I have lent him to the Lord. As long as he lives, he is lent to the Lord.” She left the little boy with Eli, to grow up serving at the tabernacle.""", ("1 Samuel", 1)),
C("Hannah’s song", "Hannah praised God in a song that celebrates how He lifts the poor and brings down the proud.",
"""Hannah then prayed again, this time not with tears but with praise. “My heart exults in the Lord; my horn is exalted in the Lord. My mouth derides my enemies, because I rejoice in your salvation. There is none holy like the Lord: for there is none besides you; there is no rock like our God.”

Her song says that God turns things upside down. “The bows of the mighty are broken, but the feeble bind on strength. Those who were full have hired themselves out for bread, but those who were hungry have ceased to hunger. The barren has borne seven, but she who has many children is forlorn. The Lord makes poor and makes rich; he brings low and he exalts. He raises up the poor from the dust.”

The song ends by looking forward to a king, “His anointed.” Centuries later, Mary’s song after learning she would bear Jesus echoes Hannah’s words in many places.""", ("1 Samuel", 2)),
C("What happened next", "Each year Hannah made Samuel a little robe. God blessed her with five more children.",
"""Hannah did not forget her son. Every year she made him a small robe and brought it to him at Shiloh when the family went up for the sacrifice. Eli blessed Elkanah and Hannah, asking the Lord to give them children to replace the one they had given. “And the Lord visited Hannah, and she conceived and bore three sons and two daughters.”

Meanwhile, Samuel grew up in the tabernacle. The Bible says, “The boy Samuel grew in the presence of the Lord,” and that he “continued to grow both in stature and in favor with the Lord and also with man.” He became one of the greatest prophets in Israel’s history, the one who anointed both Saul and David as king.""", ("1 Samuel", 2))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Hannah brought her honest sorrow to God. She did not pretend to be fine. The Bible shows that God welcomes tears and long prayers, even when other people misunderstand them.

She also kept her promise. The child she had waited years for, she gave back to the Lord. It must have been hard to leave her little boy at Shiloh, but her trust paid off beyond what she could imagine. Her son changed a nation.

Whatever you are waiting for or asking God for, Hannah shows a way to pray: pour out your heart, trust, and leave the answer with Him. Not every prayer is answered the way hers was, but the Bible says God hears each one.""", ("1 Samuel", 1))])

LONG["lydia"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Lydia was", """Lydia was a businesswoman in the first century. She came from the city of Thyatira, in what is now western Turkey, which was known for its dyed cloth. She sold purple fabric, an expensive luxury, since real purple dye was made from sea snails and cost a great deal. She was successful enough to own a home big enough to host guests, and she ran a household that included servants or family members.

She is described as “a worshiper of God,” which means she was not Jewish by birth, but she believed in the God of Israel and prayed to Him.""", ("Acts", 16)),
("Why her story matters", """Lydia is the first person in Europe the Bible says became a follower of Jesus. Her story is a small scene in the book of Acts, but it opened the door for the message to spread to a whole new continent.""")],
cards=[
C("A call to Macedonia", "Paul had a vision of a man from Macedonia begging for help, so he crossed the sea to Europe.",
"""Around AD 50, Paul was on his second missionary journey with Silas, Timothy and Luke. They had tried to go into several places in Asia, but the Holy Spirit kept stopping them. At Troas, on the coast, Paul had a vision during the night: a man from Macedonia stood and begged, “Come over to Macedonia and help us.”

“After he had seen the vision, immediately we sought to go on into Macedonia, concluding that God had called us to preach the gospel to them.” They sailed across the Aegean Sea and came to Philippi, a leading city of Macedonia and a Roman colony. It was a big step: the Gospel was about to enter Europe.""", ("Acts", 16)),
C("Prayer by the river", "In Philippi there was no synagogue, so on the Sabbath Paul went to the river, where some women had gathered to pray.",
"""A Jewish synagogue needed ten Jewish men. Philippi apparently did not have that many, so no synagogue existed. On the Sabbath day, Paul and his friends went outside the city gate to the river, “where we supposed there was a place of prayer.” They sat down and spoke to the women who had come together.

One of them was Lydia. She was listening, and the Bible says, “The Lord opened her heart to pay attention to what was said by Paul.” That is an important line. Paul gave the message, but it was God who opened her heart. She was not talked into it by a clever speech.

She believed and was baptized, along with her whole household. Then she did something typical of a generous person: “If you have judged me to be faithful to the Lord, come to my house and stay.” The Bible says, “And she prevailed upon us.” She insisted.""", ("Acts", 16)),
C("Her house", "Lydia’s home became the base for Paul and the new church. When Paul and Silas were released from jail, they went back there.",
"""While Paul stayed in Philippi, he got into trouble. He freed a slave girl from a spirit, and her owners, who made money from her fortune-telling, dragged Paul and Silas before the authorities. They were beaten and thrown into the inner prison with their feet in stocks. At midnight they were praying and singing hymns when an earthquake shook the prison doors open. The jailer, about to kill himself in fear, ended up believing and being baptized with his whole family.

When the officials released them the next morning, the Bible says, “They went out of the prison and visited Lydia. And when they had seen the brothers, they encouraged them and departed.” Lydia’s home had become the meeting place for the new church. It is likely that early Christians in Philippi gathered there for worship and meals.""", ("Acts", 16)),
C("The church at Philippi", "Paul later wrote the church a warm letter, thanking them for their generosity.",
"""A few years later, Paul wrote a letter to the church in Philippi, now the New Testament book of Philippians. It is one of his warmest letters, full of joy even though he was in prison when he wrote it. He tells them, “I thank my God in all my remembrance of you.”

He also thanks them for supporting him: “You Philippians yourselves know that in the beginning of the gospel, when I left Macedonia, no church entered into partnership with me in giving and receiving, except you only. Even in Thessalonica you sent me help for my needs once and again.”

The Bible does not say exactly who gave what, but a church that began in a successful businesswoman’s house, and was open-handed from the beginning, fits what we know about Lydia.""", ("Philippians", 4))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Lydia’s story shows that faith often starts with a quiet hearing. There was no miracle, no crowd, no dramatic moment. A woman went to pray by a river, listened, and God opened her heart.

It also shows what a changed life looks like. Lydia’s first response was to open her home. She used what she had, her business, her house and her influence, for the work of God. The first church in Europe began in a living room.

You do not need a big platform to matter in God’s plan. Many people in the Bible are remembered because of one act of faith and welcome, and Lydia is one of them.""", ("Acts", 16))])

LONG["rahab"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Rahab was", """Rahab lived in Jericho, a walled city in the Jordan valley, about 1400 BC. Her house was built into the city wall. The Bible calls her a prostitute. That means she was part of a pagan city and lived a life God’s law did not approve of. She was an outsider to Israel in every way: wrong nation, wrong religion, wrong reputation.

Yet she appears in the Bible as a woman of great faith, and she is named in the family line of Jesus.""", ("Joshua", 2)),
("Why her story matters", """Rahab’s story shows that God’s mercy reaches people no one else would choose. It also shows what faith looks like when it has to act.""")],
cards=[
C("Two spies at her door", "Joshua sent two men to look at Jericho, and they stayed at Rahab’s house.",
"""Israel had finally arrived at the east bank of the Jordan River after forty years in the wilderness. Moses had died, and Joshua was now the leader. Joshua secretly sent two spies across the river to look at the land, especially Jericho, the first big city in their way.

The two men went to Rahab’s house. Maybe it was a place where strangers would not be noticed. But someone saw them, and the king of Jericho was told. He sent a message to Rahab: “Bring out the men who have come to you, who entered your house, for they have come to search out all the land.”

This was dangerous. If she was caught hiding enemies, she could be killed. If she gave them up, they would die.""", ("Joshua", 2)),
C("Hiding the spies", "Rahab hid the men under stalks of flax on her roof and sent the king’s men on a false trail.",
"""Rahab had already taken the two men up to the roof and hidden them under stalks of flax that she had laid out there. To the king’s messengers, she said, “True, the men came to me, but I did not know where they were from. And when the gate was about to be closed at dark, the men went out. I do not know where the men went. Pursue them quickly, for you will overtake them.” The messengers left, and the city gate was shut behind them.

The Bible’s later comments on Rahab’s lie are not about the lie itself. They praise what she did: she protected God’s servants at great risk to herself.""", ("Hebrews", 11)),
C("A confession of faith", "Rahab told the spies she knew the Lord had given them the land, and asked them to spare her family.",
"""Before the men went to sleep, Rahab went up to the roof and said: “I know that the Lord has given you the land, and that the fear of you has fallen upon us. All the inhabitants of the land melt away before you. For we have heard how the Lord dried up the water of the Red Sea before you when you came out of Egypt... And as soon as we heard it, our hearts melted, and there was no spirit left in any man because of you, for the Lord your God, he is God in the heavens above and on the earth beneath.”

Notice what she says: they had heard the stories from forty years before, and the people of Jericho were terrified, but only Rahab drew the right conclusion. She said that the Lord is God. Then she asked for a favor: “Please swear to me by the Lord that, as I have dealt kindly with you, you also will deal kindly with my father’s house.” The spies agreed: “Our life for yours!” They told her to tie a scarlet cord in her window and keep her whole family inside her house. Then she let the men down by a rope through the window, since her house was on the wall.""", ("Joshua", 2)),
C("When the walls fell", "Jericho’s walls fell after Israel marched around the city for seven days. Rahab and her family were saved.",
"""A few weeks later Israel crossed the Jordan. God told Joshua to march the army around Jericho once a day for six days, with priests carrying the ark and blowing trumpets. On the seventh day they marched around seven times. Then the priests blew the trumpets, the people shouted, and the walls of the city fell flat.

Before the attack, Joshua told the two spies, “Go into the prostitute’s house and bring out from there the woman and all who belong to her, as you swore to her.” They brought out Rahab, her father, mother, brothers and all her family. The Bible says she “has lived in Israel to this day.”""", ("Joshua", 6)),
C("Rahab in the family of Jesus", "Rahab joined Israel, and she is listed as an ancestor of King David and of Jesus.",
"""The story does not end at Jericho. Matthew’s Gospel opens with Jesus’ family tree. In it, we read: “Salmon the father of Boaz by Rahab.” Rahab married an Israelite named Salmon, and their son was Boaz, the kind man who later married Ruth. Boaz’s grandson was Jesse, and Jesse’s son was King David. And from David came Jesus.

So the woman from Jericho became part of the line of the Messiah. Matthew, who mentions only four women in his genealogy besides Mary, names Rahab among them. The New Testament also praises her twice. The letter to the Hebrews says, “By faith Rahab the prostitute did not perish with those who were disobedient, because she had given a friendly welcome to the spies.” James says she was shown to be right with God by what she did.""", ("Matthew", 1))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Rahab is proof that no one is too far from God. Her past, her city and her reputation were all against her, yet she is remembered for her faith, not her failures. The Bible says what matters is not where you came from, but what you do with what you have heard about God.

Her faith was practical. She did not just believe in her heart; she risked her life and her home. And the scarlet cord on her window has long reminded people of how God saves: those inside the marked house were spared.

If you have ever felt you are too far gone to belong, Rahab is the answer. God put a woman from the wrong side of the wall into the family tree of His own Son.""", ("Hebrews", 11))])

LONG["samuel"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Samuel was", """Samuel was the last of Israel’s judges and one of its greatest prophets, living about 1100 to 1020 BC. He was born in answer to his mother Hannah’s prayer, and she gave him to serve at the tabernacle in Shiloh when he was a small child. He grew up with the old priest Eli, in a time when the nation was in trouble.

Samuel is the link between two eras: the rough, unstable time of the judges, and the time of the kings. He anointed Israel’s first two kings, Saul and David.""", ("1 Samuel", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """Samuel’s life is about listening to God and speaking His word, even when it is hard. It is also about what happens when a nation asks for a king instead of trusting God.""")],
cards=[
C("The voice in the night", "As a boy, Samuel heard a voice call his name. He thought it was Eli, but it was the Lord.",
"""In those days, “the word of the Lord was rare; there was no frequent vision.” Eli was old, almost blind. His two sons, Hophni and Phinehas, were priests, but they were corrupt: they stole from the offerings and mistreated the people.

One night, Samuel was lying down in the tabernacle near the ark of God when he heard a voice: “Samuel!” He ran to Eli, “Here I am, for you called me.” “I did not call; lie down again.” It happened a second time, then a third. Then Eli understood that the Lord was calling the boy. He told Samuel, “Go, lie down, and if he calls you, you shall say, ‘Speak, Lord, for your servant hears.’”

Samuel did. The Lord told him a hard message: He was about to judge Eli’s family, because Eli’s sons were wicked and Eli had not stopped them. In the morning Samuel was afraid to tell Eli. Eli made him say it all. “It is the Lord. Let him do what seems good to him.” From then on, the Bible says, “the Lord was with him and let none of his words fall to the ground.” All Israel knew that Samuel was a prophet.""", ("1 Samuel", 3)),
C("The ark is lost", "Israel lost a battle with the Philistines and the ark of God was captured. Eli and his sons died.",
"""Some years later, Israel went to war with the Philistines and lost about 4,000 men. The elders decided to bring the ark of the covenant from Shiloh into the camp, as though it were a good luck charm. They shouted so loudly the ground shook. But the Philistines won an even bigger victory, killing 30,000 Israelite soldiers, killing Eli’s two sons and capturing the ark itself.

A messenger ran to Shiloh with the news. Eli, 98 years old and blind, was sitting by the gate. When he heard the ark had been taken, he fell backward off his seat, broke his neck and died. His daughter-in-law, in labor, named her newborn son Ichabod, meaning “the glory has departed from Israel.”

But the Philistines found the ark was no trophy. Wherever they put it, they suffered plagues, and after seven months they sent it back to Israel.""", ("1 Samuel", 4)),
C("Ebenezer", "Twenty years later, Samuel called Israel to return to the Lord, and God gave them victory over the Philistines.",
"""For twenty years Israel was oppressed. Then Samuel told the people: “If you are returning to the Lord with all your heart, then put away the foreign gods and the Ashtaroth from among you and direct your heart to the Lord and serve him only, and he will deliver you out of the hand of the Philistines.” They did.

Samuel gathered everyone at Mizpah and prayed for them. When the Philistines came up to attack, the Lord thundered with a great voice against them, and they were thrown into confusion. Israel chased them and won.

Afterward Samuel took a stone and set it up between Mizpah and Shen. He named it Ebenezer, which means “stone of help,” and said: “Till now the Lord has helped us.” Samuel became a judge who traveled every year to Bethel, Gilgal and Mizpah, and then returned home to Ramah to settle disputes and build an altar.""", ("1 Samuel", 7)),
C("“Give us a king”", "When Samuel was old, the people demanded a king. God told Samuel to give them one and chose Saul.",
"""As Samuel grew old, he made his sons judges, but they took bribes and did not follow his example. The elders came to Samuel and said, “Appoint for us a king to judge us like all the nations.” Samuel was upset. But the Lord said to him, “Obey the voice of the people... for they have not rejected you, but they have rejected me from being king over them.”

Samuel warned them what a king would do: take their sons for the army, their daughters to work in the palace, their best fields and a tenth of everything they had. They said, “No! But there shall be a king over us, that we also may be like all the nations.”

God led Samuel to a tall young man named Saul. Samuel poured oil on his head and kissed him: “Has not the Lord anointed you to be prince over his people Israel?” He presented Saul before all Israel at Mizpah, and the people shouted, “Long live the king!”""", ("1 Samuel", 8)),
C("Saul rejected, David anointed", "Saul disobeyed God. God sent Samuel to Bethlehem to anoint a shepherd boy named David.",
"""Saul began well, but he did not stay faithful. Twice he disobeyed God’s instructions. The second time, he spared the best of the Amalekites’ livestock, when God had told him to destroy everything. When Samuel confronted him, Saul said he saved the animals to sacrifice to the Lord. Samuel answered: “Has the Lord as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices as in obeying the voice of the Lord? Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice.” Then he told Saul that the Lord had rejected him as king.

Samuel mourned for Saul. God said, “How long will you grieve over Saul? Fill your horn with oil and go. I will send you to Jesse of Bethlehem, for I have provided for myself a king among his sons.” Samuel looked at Jesse’s oldest, tall and impressive Eliab, and thought, “Surely the Lord’s anointed is before him.” God said, “Do not look on his appearance... For the Lord sees not as man sees: man looks on the outward appearance, but the Lord looks on the heart.”

Seven sons passed by, and none was chosen. “Are all your sons here?” “There remains yet the youngest, but behold, he is keeping the sheep.” David was brought in, and God said, “Arise, anoint him, for this is he.” Samuel anointed him in front of his brothers, and the Spirit of the Lord rushed upon David.""", ("1 Samuel", 16))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Samuel’s answer to God as a boy is a good prayer for anyone: “Speak, for your servant is listening.” He spent his whole life saying it. He spoke hard words to Eli, to Israel and to Saul, and none of what he said fell to the ground.

The story also shows that God cares about the heart. People saw tall Saul and tall Eliab. God saw David, a shepherd nobody had thought to invite. When we judge by appearance, we often miss what God is doing.

Finally, “Till now the Lord has helped us” is a good stone to set up in our own lives. Looking back and remembering how God has helped is one way to trust Him for what comes next.""", ("1 Samuel", 7))])
